  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe axis of the upper-level trough will still be oriented along the \nMississippi River at the start of the extended period on Tuesday, \nwith the surface high holding strong over the Southeast. With this \npattern holding through at least Thursday, the early week heat will \nonly continue. One variable that may fluctuate is dew points, as \ncontinuous southerly flow with uninhibited Gulf access will gradually \nadvect greater moisture into the area. Assuming high daily high \ntemperatures top out in the upper 90s to around 100 degrees, this \nwould lead to an uptick in heat index values. While this is the \ncurrent NBM solution, an increase in humidity would lead to a couple-\ndegree decrease in temperatures. Additionally, there is a strong \nconsensus among guidance for surface flow to be out of the \nsouthwest daily, which would lower dew points and boost temperatures \nthanks to downsloping off the Ozarks. Therefore, confidence remains \nlow to medium in heat index magnitude as we get later into the week. \n\nConfidence decreases in our local weather Friday into the weekend, \nas guidance notably diverges on the phasing of the upper-level \nridge. While there is some consensus on the ridge amplifying, where \nexactly this occurs across the CONUS varies among deterministic \nguidance and ensemble clusters. If the ridge axis remains generally \nover the Midwest, then we will see a continuation of the heat into \nthe weekend. If the ridge amplifies over the Plains as some guidance \nsuggests, then the CWA will be beneath northwesterly flow and \ntemperatures will decrease. The current forecast splits the \ndifferences with warm by gradually decreasing temperatures through \nthe weekend.",
